'''''Malephora''''' is a genus of [[succulent plant]]s in the [[Aizoaceae|ice plant family]]. There are 13<ref>[http://www.efloras.org/florataxon.aspx?flora_id=1&taxon_id=119533 Flora of North America]</ref> to 17<ref>[http://www.plantzafrica.com/plantklm/malephora.htm South African National Biodiversity Institute]</ref> species in the genus, many of which are known commonly as '''mesembs'''. They are native to Namibia and the [[Cape Provinces]] and [[Free State (province)|Free State]] of South Africa.<ref name = powo/> During the [[apartheid]] era, South African scientists used it to create a chemical poison.{{Citation needed|reason=Reliable source needed for this sentence|date=March 2016}}

These are perennial herbs used as groundcovers lined with fleshy, smooth, sometimes waxy leaves no more than {{convert|6|cm}} long. Leaves are triangular or rounded in cross-section and are arranged oppositely about the stem. The tubular flowers arise from leaf axils or at the ends of stem branches. The flowers may be several centimetres wide. They have up to 65 narrow petals in bright shades of yellow, orange, pink or purple. The center of the flower has whorls of up to 150 [[stamen]]s. The fruit is a capsule that opens when it becomes wet, releasing the many seeds.

Some species are used as [[ornamental plant]]s, particularly ''[[Malephora crocea]]''.

